WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.300
This completion exercise asks you to filter JSONB
notes where follow_up is

00:00:04.325 --> 00:00:09.862
true The SELECT portion already extracts the patient
ID symptom and severity

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.688
The missing piece is inside the WHERE condition We
need to tell

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:20.338
PostgreSQL which JSON key contains the Boolean
follow-up flag Replace the blank

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:26.012
with the quoted key name follow_up Because the
arrow-arrow operator extracts text

00:00:26.225 --> 00:00:30.088
the expression then casts that text to Boolean using
double colon boolean

00:00:30.975 --> 00:00:35.675
Finally it compares the result with true After you
complete the key

00:00:35.925 --> 00:00:40.825
run the query Every returned note should represent a
case where follow_up

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:46.862
is true This example combines several ideas in one
short condition First

00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:53.212
access a JSON key Second extract the value as text
Third

00:00:53.362 --> 00:00:57.238
cast it to the data type you need Fourth compare it
with

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:01.788
the desired value If the syntax feels unusual read
it from the

00:01:01.812 --> 00:01:07.362
inside out Start with the note column extract
follow_up convert that value

00:01:07.388 --> 00:01:11.162
to Boolean and then ask whether it equals true The
more you

00:01:11.188 --> 00:01:16.188
work with JSONB the more natural these operators
become For now focus

00:01:16.212 --> 00:01:19.425
on understanding the sequence rather than memorizing
every symbol
